{"summary": "I wrote `transpose` in `transpose.py`, and it gives the right output for the examples I tried.

It works through the rows from the bottom up and pads each one with spaces on the right until it is at least as long as every row below it. Then it reads the rows column by column. This adds left padding in the output where it's needed, never adds trailing padding, and keeps any spaces that were in the original text.

I ran it by hand on `ABC\nDE` (gives `AD\nBE\nC`), `AB\nDEF` (gives `AD\nBE\n F`), an empty string, and a triangle-shaped input; all came out as expected. The project has no test files, so I couldn't run the official tests.", "outputs": ["transpose.py"]}
